<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guru Nanak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1469">1469</a>–<a href="/years/1539">1539</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nanak, Baba Nanak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guru Nanak was the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a> and the first of its ten gurus, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in 1469. After a transforming religious experience he travelled widely, by tradition as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, teaching devotion to one formless God, the equality of all people regardless of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, and honest work and shared food. He settled at Kartarpur, where a community of disciples, the first Sikhs, gathered around him; his hymns open the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>, Sikhism's scripture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-nanak.jpg" alt="Guru Nanak" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amritpal Singh Mann. Painted by Jaimal Singh Naqqash (1860–1916), Mehtab Singh Naqqash (1871–1940), and Hukum Singh. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mural_painting_of_Guru_Nanak_from_Gurdwara_Baba_Atal_Rai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founder, <b>Guru Nanak</b>, was born in 1469 into a Hindu family of the Khatri caste at Talwandi, now Nankana Sahib in Pakistan, in the Punjab, a land where Hindu and Muslim traditions met.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Punjab <b>Guru Nanak</b>, the founder of Sikhism, taught devotion to one formless God in terms close to theirs, though Sikh tradition regards his teaching as a revelation of its own.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>…in the 12th century Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir settled in the valley of Lalish, united the scattered Yazidi tribes and gave Yazidism its lasting form; <b>Guru Nanak</b> began his mission after an experience at the river Bein around 1499; the Báb declared his mission in Shiraz in 1844.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>It is mostly the writing of <b>Guru Nanak</b> and was put together by Guru Angad: it begins with the Mool Mantra, continues with 38 paudis (stanzas) in different poetic metres, and ends with a Salok by Guru Angad.</blockquote><a href="/songs/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a></li><li><blockquote>He was the first of <b>Guru Nanak</b>'s successors to rekindle ties with Nanak's son Sri Chand, after a long estrangement between mainstream Sikhs and the Udasis.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Amar Das became Sikh Guru at the age of 73, the third of the gurus who established the religion over about two and a half centuries, a span beginning in 1469 with the birth of <b>Guru Nanak</b>, the founder of Sikhism.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-26">26 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nankana Sahib, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in Kartarpur. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.45,73.71&amp;year=1514" aria-label="Nankana Sahib on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6452 1018 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guru Nanak"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6902 1288h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6902 1288h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.45,73.71&amp;year=1514">What was here in 1514? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
